ntdll: Route NtWaitForSingleObject through msync/esync as well.

NtWaitForSingleObject is not a wrapper around NtWaitForMultipleObjects in
wine 11: it has its own body which goes straight to inproc_wait() and then to
the server select.  0004 hooks NtWaitForMultipleObjects, NtSignalAndWait-
ForSingleObject and NtDelayExecution but not this one, so a handle created by
msync_create_semaphore() or its event and mutex siblings reaches a wait path
that has no object for it and fails immediately.

Measured against the wine-11.14 build with WINEMSYNC=1, using tests/synctest.c
which asserts an invariant per primitive rather than timing a loop:

  WaitAll times out when unsignalled     PASS     <- NtWaitForMultipleObjects
  WaitAny returns the signalled one      PASS     <- hooked, works
  WaitAll returns when both signalled    PASS
  manual reset event stays signalled     FAIL     <- WaitForSingleObject
  manual reset event resets              FAIL     <- unhooked, fails
  abandoned mutex reported               FAIL
  semaphore+mutex count exact            FAIL     80000 ops in 2 ms, counter 0

Every multi-object case passed and every single-object case failed, which is
the signature of exactly one missing hook.  The workers return on the first
non-WAIT_OBJECT_0, so a counter of 0 means the very first wait failed rather
than the loop running wrong.

The hook goes ahead of inproc_wait() to match where 0004 placed the one in
NtWaitForMultipleObjects, and uses the same STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED fallthrough
so a handle the backend does not own still reaches the normal path.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
@@ -2443,6 +2443,21 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtWaitForSingleObject( HANDLE handle, BOOLEAN alertable, const L

     TRACE( "handle %p, alertable %u, timeout %s\n", handle, alertable, debugstr_timeout(timeout) );

+    /* this is not a wrapper around NtWaitForMultipleObjects, so hooking that
+     * one is not enough: without this an msync or esync backed handle reaches
+     * the server wait path, which has no object for it, and fails immediately */
+    if (do_msync())
+    {
+        NTSTATUS status = msync_wait_objects( 1, &handle, TRUE, alertable, timeout );
+        if (status != STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED) return status;
+    }
+
+    if (do_esync())
+    {
+        NTSTATUS status = esync_wait_objects( 1, &handle, TRUE, alertable, timeout );
+        if (status != STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED) return status;
+    }
+
     if ((ret = inproc_wait( 1, &handle, WaitAny, alertable, timeout )) != STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED)
     {
         TRACE( "-> %#x\n", ret );
